<item>
  <title>My scorer gave a perfect extractor 0 out of 6</title>
  <dc:creator>Santosh Kumar Panda</dc:creator>
  <link>https://mediansant.github.io/myblog/posts/eval-lied/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 





<p>The first time I scored my contract extractor, it got zero out of six fields right on the first contract.</p>
<p>Every one of the six was correct.</p>
<p>The model had done its job. My scorer hadn’t. That moment set the theme for the whole project: when you build with LLMs, the evaluation is code too, and a wrong eval is worse than no eval, because it sends you off “fixing” a prompt that isn’t broken.</p>
<p>This post covers what I built, the ten ways my eval lied to me, what the real numbers looked like once it stopped lying, and why most failures weren’t the model’s fault. You can explore every failure yourself further down.</p>
<section id="the-setup" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-setup">The setup</h2>
<p>The task: take messy commercial contracts in, and return clean, structured JSON records out, with quality measured per field.</p>
<p>I used <a href="https://www.atticusprojectai.org/cuad">CUAD</a>, a public set of commercial contracts from SEC filings, labelled by legal experts. Having labels from day one meant I could measure from the start instead of eyeballing outputs.</p>
