<p>Docling turns each PDF into markdown. A Pydantic model defines the record, and OpenAI’s structured outputs constrain the model’s response to that schema, so I always get valid, typed JSON back. Three schema choices did most of the work:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Absence is explicit.</strong> Fields are <code>str | None</code> with no default, so the model has to decide “present or not” every time instead of skipping the field.</li>
<li><strong>Every value comes with evidence.</strong> A verbatim quote from the contract, which I can check is really in the source.</li>
<li><strong>The field descriptions are the prompt.</strong> The model reads them, so I wrote them like instructions to a junior paralegal.</li>
</ul>
<p>One thing to be clear about: structured outputs guarantee the <em>shape</em> of the answer, not its truth. A perfectly valid record can still hold the wrong date. That’s what the evaluation is for.</p>
</section>
<section id="ten-ways-my-eval-lied" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="ten-ways-my-eval-lied">Ten ways my eval lied</h2>
<p>Here’s the full list. Every one of these produced output that looked plausible, which is exactly what made them dangerous.</p>
<ol type="1">
<li><strong>I compared against the wrong column.</strong> CUAD has a raw clause-text column and a normalised answer column for each field. I scored against the raw text, so “Delaware” never matched a full sentence about Delaware law.</li>
<li><strong>I used exact string equality for every field.</strong> “2007-04-06” doesn’t equal “April 6, 2007”.</li>
<li><strong>“Absent” had five spellings.</strong> <code>None</code>, <code>NaN</code>, <code>""</code>, <code>[]</code> and <code>False</code> all mean “not in the contract”, but they didn’t compare equal to each other, so correct absences were marked wrong.</li>
<li><strong>A column lookup silently missed.</strong> I searched for a column starting with “party”. The column is called “Parties”. The ground truth for parties was empty for all twenty contracts.</li>
<li><strong>True negatives counted as hits.</strong> When both the model and the label said “no”, my code counted a true positive. That inflated recall on the two yes/no fields.</li>
<li><strong>Wrong values only counted as misses.</strong> If the model said Illinois and the label said New York, I counted a false negative but not a false positive, so precision showed a perfect 1.00 alongside three wrong answers.</li>
<li><strong>The join between outputs and labels was a substring match.</strong> A filename ending “…Agreement” could match one ending “…Agreement3” and score a contract against another contract’s labels.</li>
<li><strong>Contracts were dropped silently.</strong> A <code>continue</code> skipped contracts while the header still said “20 contracts”.</li>
<li><strong>My name cleaning merged entities.</strong> Stripping everything in brackets turned “TOM Online (BVI) Limited” into “tom online limited”.</li>
<li><strong>I read a stale results file.</strong> An old output got re-shared as new. The numbers looked familiar because they were the old ones.</li>
</ol>
<p>The fixes are boring, and that’s the point. One matcher per field type. One function that decides what “absent” means. An explicit map from field to column that raises an error if a column is missing. Exact joins. A check that every field’s outcomes add up to the number of contracts scored. And unit tests built from the awkward real values I’d just been burned by.</p>
</section>
